is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF area-type configuration on the ABR (r2), specifically the area 4 adjacency toward r10 on r2’s eth2 (21.1.1.1) / r10’s eth0 (21.1.1.2).

Mechanism: OSPF requires neighbors to agree on the “stub” flag for an area. If one side treats an area as stub and the other as a normal area, the adjacency never comes up (it drops during negotiation), so the internal router (r10) loses summaries/default from the ABR and thus all routes beyond its area.

Localization: r10 shows no OSPF neighbors at all (show ip ospf neighbor is empty). r2 shows a neighbor only on eth1 (area 0) and none on eth2 (area 4). r10’s OSPF process explicitly marks “Area ID: 0.0.0.4 (Stub)”, while r2’s OSPF shows Area 0.0.0.4 without the stub designation, and r2’s running-config lacks any “area 4 stub” statement. This confirms an area-type mismatch on the r2–r10 link (I/21.0.0.0/8). OSPF is running on r2 (it’s an ABR with an active neighbor in area 0), so there is no evidence of an ospfd crash.

Root cause: A recent configuration change on r2 removed the “area 4 stub” setting, creating an OSPF area-type mismatch with r10.

Corrective fix: Do not restart ospfd. Instead, align area types. Recommended: on r2, add “area 4 stub” under router ospf (vtysh: conf t; router ospf; area 4 stub; end; write). Alternatively, remove “area 4 stub” from r10 to make area 4 normal on both sides. After matching, the r2–r10 adjacency will form and r10 will regain summarized routes/default to the backbone. === END ===